WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.049
She was originally introduced as the daughter
of Batman and Catwoman, and made her way back

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.360
from being dead not once, but twice.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.280
Let’s take a look at all the incarnations
of the Huntress.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.160
Like a lot of the most confusing pieces of
the DC Universe, the Huntress has her roots

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.160
in Earth-2.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.120
In her case, though, it's not just a simple
story of crossing dimensions.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.630
More than anyone else, she shows how an attempt
to solve a storytelling problem that didn't

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:29.660
really matter gave DC the chance to take their
characters in some very unexpected directions.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:34.720
It starts in the 1960s, when DC decided they
needed to explain why there were new characters

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.020
with the same names as a few of the old ones.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.600
The extremely simple solution was to dive
headfirst into the idea of parallel worlds.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.430
In the pages of Flash, a parallel world called
Earth-2 was created as a home for all of the

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.560
original versions of the DC superheroes.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.079
Of course, the idea of Earth-2 also created
a few new problems, like how some of the Earth-2

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.220
heroes existed as fictional comic book characters
on Earth-1.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.760
Barry Allen, for example, chose to name himself
"The Flash" because he'd read the same comics

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.440
about the original Flash, Jay Garrick, that
were published in the real world.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.340
Apparently nobody ever read the ones about
how Batman and Superman were really Bruce

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.620
Wayne and Clark Kent.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.980
One of the most interesting things about Earth-2
was that DC now had versions of its most popular

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:19.679
characters that could change in ways the ones
in the main stories never could.

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:23.999
Earth-2 Superman was happily married to Lois
Lane, while his Earth-1 counterpart was still

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.620
refusing to settle down as he waged his never-ending
battle against evil.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.560
The same went for the Batman family.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.320
Dick Grayson grew up, keeping his identity
as Robin into adulthood, and Bruce Wayne retired

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.040
from crime-fighting and married Selina Kyle,
better known as Catwoman.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.020
Earth-2's Batman and Catwoman would even die
after their retirement, prompting an elderly

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.380
Joker to go on a rampage after being denied
a final clash with his arch-nemesis.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.840
Long before that, however, Bruce and Selina
had a child together.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.440
Her name was Helena Wayne, and as you might
expect, it wasn't long before she joined the

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.040
family business.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.520
As a crimefighter, she took the name Huntress
and wore a costume that combined Batman's

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.280
pointy-eared look with Catwoman's traditional
purple.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.720
What might be surprising, though, is that
she wasn't the kind of prominent character

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.460
you'd expect Batman and Catwoman's daughter
to become.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.660
While she starred in a few stories and fought
crime alongside the grown-up Robin as the

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.310
"New Dynamic Duo," Helena Wayne's most prominent
appearances came when she took an interdimensional

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.220
vacation to Earth-1 to spend some time with
that world's version of her dad.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.540
Of course, meeting up with the alternate reality
daughter you fathered with a supervillain

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.900
is always going to be a little weird, but,
you know.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.200
It's the DC Universe.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.840
You get used to stuff like that.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:37.420
By the mid '80s, DC's Multiverse had expanded
well beyond Earth-2 into a whole roster of

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.860
alternate worlds built around different ideas.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.159
"One where the Nazis won World War II, one
where Kennedy was never assassinated…”

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.020
“Oh yeah, one where all of us are evil.”

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:48.780
“Been there, it sucks."

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:53.739
In 1985, they tried to clean it all up with
a 12-issue event called Crisis on Infinite

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:54.380
Earths.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.920
The ads for the series claimed that "worlds
will live, worlds will die, and the DC Universe

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:03.120
will never be the same," and while comic books
have often featured some taglines that went

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.609
way over the top, this one actually lived
up to it.

00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:09.370
The main effect of the story was that the
Multiverse of parallel worlds was obliterated

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:11.920
and streamlined into a single unified timeline.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.540
As a result, there was no more Earth-2 Batman,
because there was no more Earth-2 to be the

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:17.220
Batman of.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.300
If Earth-2 didn't exist, then neither did
Helena Wayne.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:24.200
It's worth noting that Huntress was one of
the few characters to survive the initial

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:28.510
destruction of her home dimension, finding
herself stranded alongside her Earth's Robin

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.100
on a strange world with no record of her existence.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.920
With nothing left for her in the universe,
she joined Robin on a final, fatal mission.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.320
The final indignity?

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.880
The story specifically states that her body
and Robin's were never recovered after their

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:41.420
deaths.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.720
Here's the thing about superhero comics: nothing
ever really goes away, especially when it's

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.040
tied to a character as prominent as Batman.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.100
With that in mind, it's no surprise that DC
brought back the Huntress only a few years

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.020
after Crisis.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.640
What is surprising is that they completely
reinvented the character in almost every aspect,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.959
aside from her costume and her first name
and even the costume underwent some radical

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.340
changes before too long.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.879
Instead of being Batman's daughter, the post-Crisis
Huntress was something a little closer to

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.780
being DC's take on the Punisher.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.959
Helena Bertinelli was the youngest member
of one of Gotham City's most prominent mobster

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:19.209
families in the years before Batman arrived,
and organized crime was mostly replaced by

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.760
evil clowns and mad scientists with ice guns.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.900
When she was only eight years old, Helena's
entire family was murdered in front of her

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.440
by a hitman who left her alive as the only
survivor.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.740
Helena was taken in by her family in Sicily,
who offered to train her so that she could

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.720
get her revenge, something that she took to
very well.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.000
When she returned to Gotham, however, she
saw Batman in action, and realized that if

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.550
she really wanted to strike terror into the
hearts of the mobsters who had targeted her

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.200
family, she should follow in his footsteps
as a vigilante.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.990
With that, she took the identity of the Huntress,
although that wasn't her only calling.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.450
While she was wealthy enough to live off of
her family's ill-gotten gains, she actually

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.660
kept a day job as a school teacher, something
that would have more prominence in other versions

00:04:58.660 --> 00:04:59.440
of the character.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:04.160
You don't really get to be a vigilante in
Gotham City without attracting some attention

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.270
from the vigilante in Gotham City.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.680
Sure enough, the new Huntress soon encountered
Batman.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:13.140
The big sticking point between the two characters
comes from the way they deal with their enemies.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.780
Most versions of Batman famously have a code
against killing.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.880
"You finally learned to do what is necessary."

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.120
"I won't kill you."

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.980
The Huntress, on the other hand, has no problem
at all sending her opponents off to their

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:25.140
final reward.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.660
Still, Batman didn't set out to take the Huntress
down.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.680
After all, if there's one person who understands
the intense trauma of watching your family

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:35.270
get gunned down in front of you, and the anger
that can lead you to put on a costume and

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:38.280
wage war against all criminals by night, it's
that guy.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.580
Still, he didn't approve of her methods, but
the solution he came up with for this particular

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.850
problem wasn't the one that anyone expected.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.640
He let her join the Justice League.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.920
Batman's hope was that membership in the JLA,
surrounded by paragons of virtue like Superman

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.500
and Wonder Woman, would be a good influence
on the Huntress and show her a different way

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:56.480
of fighting crime.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:57.680
It did not work.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.000
When Batman found her getting ready to execute
a helpless supervillain at the climax of one

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.520
of the League's biggest crises, he booted
her out of the league in disappointment.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.240
Helena Bertinelli's relationship with the
rest of the Batman family is a little complicated.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:15.280
We've already been through her rocky interactions
with the Dark Knight himself, but his sidekicks

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.640
tend to have a very different perspective
on the Huntress.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:22.720
Tim Drake, for instance, had the sort of relationship
you'd expect from a super-smart teenage overachiever

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:23.920
and a world-weary teacher.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.940
Her relationship with Dick Grayson, better
known as Nightwing, is… a little more mature.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.800
When he first encountered the Huntress, they
had immediate chemistry, and they quickly

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.120
found themselves in a team-up with benefits.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.030
An actual relationship didn't blossom out
of that encounter, though, partly because

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:43.140
of the time that Huntress accidentally shot
Batman with a crossbow in front of Nightwing.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:47.460
There are definitely exceptions, but using
medieval weaponry on someone's beloved father

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.160
figure is going to be a dealbreaker.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.100
Even more than Batman himself, Helena Bertinelli's
most complicated relationship within the Dark

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.860
Knight's group of assorted sidekicks was with
Barbara Gordon.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:02.060
And before you start wondering, it actually
had very little to do with Barbara's on-again,

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.620
off-again relationship with Dick Grayson.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:08.000
Instead, the biggest source of conflict came
from what was essentially the superheroic

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:09.860
equivalent of copyright infringement.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:14.540
It happened in No Man's Land, a year-long
crossover event where a devastating earthquake

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:17.280
left Gotham City broken and abandoned by the
government.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:22.340
After unsuccessfully campaigning to save the
city, Bruce Wayne was left dejected and depressed,

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:26.330
and even contemplated abandoning the city
himself, while supervillains carved Gotham

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:28.620
into their own little Fury Road territories.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.960
While Batman was gone, a mysterious new Bat-person
appeared, who eventually turned out to be

00:07:32.961 --> 00:07:37.180
the Huntress, using Batman's identity and
iconography to give hope to the remaining

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:37.900
citizens.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.820
When Batman returned, he gave her his approval
to continue acting as the ersatz Bagirl, which

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.200
didn't sit well with Barbara.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:48.930
Babs had been forced to give up being Batgirl
after being paralyzed by the Joker, and was

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:52.920
infuriated by the idea of Batman giving away
something that she still thought of as a big

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:56.520
part of her identity without even telling
her who was under the mask.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.980
Eventually, around the same time that she
was fired from the JLA, Huntress gave up the

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:06.090
Batgirl identity voluntarily, but No Man's
Land also provided her with a measure of redemption

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.320
for what Batman had seen as her failings.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:12.220
When the Joker launched a horrifying attempt
to murder every baby born in Gotham during

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:16.320
the No Man's Land year, Huntress made a heroic
last stand to protect the kids.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:20.810
She wound up fighting a small army of Joker
impostors, and was shot by the real Joker

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:22.440
multiple times in the process.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.420
Perhaps surprisingly, she survived the experience.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.920
After the injuries she suffered in No Man's
Land, Helena Bertinelli made a full recovery,

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.580
and so did her relationship with Barbara Gordon...
for a while.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:38.620
As Oracle, Barbara founded a team of her own
called the Birds of Prey, coordinating missions

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:40.820
with other superheroines as her operatives.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:45.860
Having felt aimless for years after her experiences
in No Man's Land, the Huntress joined up alongside

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.220
Oracle and Black Canary.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:51.480
The camaraderie of the team seemed to be doing
a lot of good for Helena, allowing her to

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.500
move past the anger and desire for revenge
that had driven her since she was a child.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:57.740
Unfortunately, it was short lived.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.620
After a few adventures with the Birds, she
realized that Oracle had been sending her

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:05.780
on missions designed to teach her lessons
and push her towards self-improvement, treating

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.920
her the same way that a teacher would handle
a problem student.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:13.440
Infuriated at being manipulated by yet another
member of the Batman family who seemed to

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.020
want to mold her into what they wanted her
to be, she ditched the team and left.

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:23.880
In 2002, the WB launched a Birds of Prey TV
show, loosely and that's the key word there

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:25.640
based on comic of the same name.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:26.950
"Who the hell are you?"

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:28.000
"I’m the Huntress.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:29.300
And you're the prey."

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.180
The show focused on two heroines.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.420
The first was Barbara Gordon, played by Dina
Meyer, who had been Batgirl until she was

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:38.240
shot by the Joker, and had reinvented herself
as Oracle.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:43.180
The second, as though things weren't complicated
enough already, was Helena Kyle, played by

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:43.980
Ashley Scott.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.720
Like the original Earth-2 Huntress, she was
the daughter of Batman and Catwoman, heavily

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:51.940
implied to be the versions seen on the big
screen in Batman Returns.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:56.620
Unlike Helena Wayne, however, this version
never knew her father, with Catwoman keeping

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:58.100
her birth a secret from Batman.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:02.480
Still, after Batman's apparent death, she
took the identity of the Huntress and carried

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:07.640
on his legacy alongside Barbara, protecting
a younger version of Black Canary, Dinah Lance.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.720
The show only lasted a single 13-episode season
before it was canceled.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:16.190
It did have some interesting ideas, however,
including casting a version of Harley Quinn

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:20.010
as the main villain of the series, marking
one of the first times she'd stepped out of

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:21.190
the Joker's shadow.

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:22.360
"Very witty, Quinzel"

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.440
"Oh, please, it’s Harley.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:25.780
Harley Quinn.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:29.800
And quite soon, this city, or what’s left
of it, will be mine."

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.200
It wasn't entirely forgotten, either 17 years
after the last episode aired, and only weeks

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.310
before the Huntress made it to the big screen
in the Birds of Prey movie, Ashley Scott returned

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.380
to the role for a cameo in CW's Crisis on
Infinite Earths crossover.

